  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nRest of Today - Monday: Drier & Seasonable \n\nUpper-level ridging and surface high pressure will be the name of \nthe game for the rest of today and Monday as cloud cover this \nmorning will continue to clear, resulting in mostly sunny \nconditions. Overall, high temperatures will generally remain in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s today with a slight increase in high \ntemperatures for Monday as southerly surface flow develops into the \nregion. \n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Shower and Storm Chances Return \n\nAs we head into Tuesday, the overall synoptic pattern changes \nslightly as an upper-level shortwave trough pushes north of the \nregion with an attending surface boundary translating through the \nregion. Consequently, measurable precipitation chances increase (50-\n90%) in the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) during this \nperiod. Overall this setup does not look overly conducive for severe \nweather due to the timing of the boundary and minimal instability \n(only around 500-750 J/kg of MUCAPE in the GFS) present during this \nperiod. Although, some elongation in hodographs during this \ntimeframe does suggest somewhat favorable shear profiles. In any \ncase, fairly respectable warm cloud depths over 4km and precipitable \nwaters approaching 2 inches would suggest a fairly favorable \nenvironment for heavy rainfall. As a result, have noticed a \nshift of tails of 0 across portions of northeast Iowa and \nsouthwest Wisconsin in the EC Extreme Forecast Index. This is \nbeing generated in part due to some probabilities (10-30%) of \nover an inch of rainfall present in the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble). Some of the highest members in the \n90th to 95th percentile grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) would suggest amounts in the 2-3\" range cannot be \nruled out during this period south of the MN state line. So \nwhile the threat for severe potential is quite low confidence \nduring this period, will have to watch to see if we can \noverachieve on rainfall amounts. \n\nIn any case, with the precipitation chances, a frontal passage and \nzonal flow aloft during this period, temperatures will likely remain \non the cooler side of normal for this time of year with median high \ntemperatures in the NBM remaining generally in the 70s to lower 80s \nfor Tuesday and Wednesday. Given that much of the local area will be \non the cool side of the frontal passage come Wednesday, would not be \noverly surprising for much of the local area to struggle to reach 80 \ndegrees. \n\nThursday - Saturday: Staying Seasonable with Shower and Storm Chances\n\nTemperatures will begin to warm slightly again as surface high \npressure behind the aforementioned frontal passage will aid in \nwarming surface temperatures. However, given zonal to \nnorthwesterly flow aloft late this week, would not expect \ntemperatures to recover all that much with the NBM keeping \ntemperatures near normal. By Friday and Saturday, predictability\nin the middle and upper-level flow pattern becomes lower as the\nsynoptic pattern favors zonal to northwesterly flow with \nembedded shortwave perturbations. As a result, thinking there \nremains some chances for showers and storms at this point in the\nforecast period, however confidence is very low in details at \nthis juncture as the location of the thermal ridge, which may \npromote capping aloft, will need to be resolved as well. \nConsequently, feel the low precipitation chances (10-40%) in \nthe NBM for Friday and Saturday are appropriate at this \njuncture.",
